   "heading": "Epidemic curves",
   "segment_index": "S1.P2",
   "kind": "missing",
   "en_span": "chapters/epicurves.qmd:27-27",
   "tr_span": "chapters/epicurves.fr.qmd:25-27",
   "proposition": "The English states that the incidence2 package offers an alternative approach with easier commands, was unstable at the time of writing, and will be re-added to the chapter when stable; the French has no equivalent sentence anywhere in the introduction.",
   "reader_consequence": "A French reader is never told that incidence2 exists as an easier alternative, nor why it is commented out of the p_load() chunk immediately below, so they cannot judge whether to look for it or wait for it.",
   "confidence": "high"
  },
  {
   "heading": "Epicurves with ggplot2",
   "segment_index": "S7.P1",
   "kind": "added",
   "en_span": "chapters/epicurves.qmd:146-146",
   "tr_span": "chapters/epicurves.fr.qmd:144-144",
   "proposition": "The French opens the sentence with \"Contrairement a l'utilisation du paquet incidence2\" (unlike using the incidence2 package), asserting a contrast with incidence2; the English sentence makes no reference to incidence2 and simply states that you must manually control the aggregation and the date-axis label intervals.",
   "reader_consequence": "A French reader is pointed at an incidence2 workflow that this version of the chapter never describes and that the package chunk has commented out, so they may go looking for a comparison section that does not exist.",
   "confidence": "high"
  },
  {
   "heading": "Weekly epicurve example",
   "segment_index": "S9.P1",
   "kind": "added",
   "en_span": "chapters/epicurves.qmd:278-278",
   "tr_span": "chapters/epicurves.fr.qmd:279-279",
   "proposition": "The French bullet adds that the date-label interval can alternatively be set automatically with label = label_date_short() from the scales package; the English bullet states only that the interval is specified by date_breaks = within scale_x_date().",
   "reader_consequence": "A French reader is given an extra method at this point that the English reader only meets later in the Multi-level date labels section, so the two editions teach a different amount of material at the same step.",
   "confidence": "high"
  },
  {
   "heading": "Adjust level order",
   "segment_index": "S13.P1",
   "kind": "added",
   "en_span": "chapters/epicurves.qmd:449-449",
   "tr_span": "chapters/epicurves.fr.qmd:452-452",
   "proposition": "The French adds a sentence recommending fct_lump() for more advanced level adjustment, stating it automatically combines small categories into an \"Autre\" category; the English paragraph mentions only fct_relevel().",
   "reader_consequence": "A French reader may apply fct_lump() expecting it to be demonstrated in the code chunk below, but no chunk in the chapter uses it, and the claim about an automatic \"Autre\" category is unverified in this chapter.",
   "confidence": "high"
  },
  {
   "heading": "Date-axis labels/gridlines",
   "segment_index": "S17.P1",
   "kind": "added",
   "en_span": "chapters/epicurves.qmd:619-619",
   "tr_span": "chapters/epicurves.fr.qmd:624-624",
   "proposition": "The French bullet adds the alternative \"OU utilisez label = label_date_short() (du paquet scales) pour des etiquettes de date automatiques et efficaces\"; the English bullet describes only date_labels = and the use of \\n for a new line.",
   "reader_consequence": "A French reader receives an extra option in this reference list that the English reference list does not carry, so a reader comparing the two editions sees a different set of documented arguments for scale_x_date().",
